# Things that must never appear in a tracked file in this repo.
#
# COPY THIS TO `.personal-terms`, which is gitignored and stays on your
# own machine, and fill it in. scripts/check_personal.mjs greps every
# tracked file for these; the pre-commit hook checks the staged ones
# and `npm test` sweeps the lot. A machine with no `.personal-terms`
# skips the check and says so, so CI and anyone who clones this are
# unaffected.
#
# FORMAT: one term per line. Blank lines and lines starting with # are
# ignored. Matching is case-insensitive and is a plain substring, not
# a regular expression — so "northgate" would catch "NorthGate
# savings", and a short term like "key" would catch half the repo.
# Prefer the longest form that is still unique.
#
# NOTHING IN THIS FILE IS REAL. It is tracked, so a real bank or a
# real figure written here as an ILLUSTRATION is the leak it warns
# about. Both of the first two drafts of this file did exactly that,
# and the gate caught both — which is the best argument for it there
# is.
#
# The failure names the file, the line and the TERM, and deliberately
# does not print the matching line: copying it into a terminal, a CI
# log and a screenshot of the failure is most of the way to committing
# it anyway.
#
# WHAT BELONGS IN IT, from the sweep that caused this (2026-10-15):
#
#   - your bank and brokerage names, in every spelling you use
#   - the names you give your own accounts
#   - real balances and amounts you have pasted in as examples, in
#     every form they appear in: the bare number AND the formatted
#     one, because a comment quotes "$9,999.99" and a fixture holds
#     9999.99. (Those two are made up. This file is tracked, so a
#     real figure written here as an ILLUSTRATION would be the leak
#     it is warning about — which is how the first draft of this line
#     was written.)
#   - the names of people in your life, and their email addresses
#   - your street, your employer, your account numbers
#
# None of what it found was malicious. A bank name sat inside a CSV
# category guesser that shipped to every user and matched nothing for
# anyone else; a real checking balance was the "measured example" in
# two files; a partner's name was a test fixture. Each of them read
# perfectly naturally while it was being written, which is the whole
# problem and the whole reason for a list.
